  "query_preview": "..\nAnother round of monsoon showers and storms are currently moving \nacross the Marianas this afternoon. Frequent lightning has been seen \nin some of these thunderstorms. Locally heavy rainfall will be \npossible tonight and Thursday before shower coverage begins to taper \ndown. A lull in the monsoon pattern is expected Friday and Saturday \nas Invest 96W moves northward over the coming days, shifting the \nstronger monsoon flow northward. \n\nTowards the latter half of the weekend, model guidance shows several \npieces of vorticity ejecting eastwards across the far northern \nMarianas south of Invest 96W (or potential tropical system down the \nroad). These circulations will help reinvigorate the monsoon and \nenhance winds across the Marianas. This will bring increased changes \nfor showers and thunderstorms as well. Models continue to differ on \nwhere the strongest winds and heaviest rainfall will set up, but the \npotential for an impactful monsoon surge remains possible next week. \n\nMarine...\nCombined sea remain between 9 and 11 feet and are expected to remain \nwithin that range through Thursday. A slight decrease in seas is \nexpected Thursday night and Friday before another increase this \nweekend. A Small Craft Advisory remains in effect until early Friday \nmorning. The Small Craft Advisory will likely need to be extended \nthrough the weekend if trends continue. A High Surf Advisory remains \nin effect for west and north facing reefs and a High Risk of Rip \nCurrents remains in effect for west, north, and east facing reefs \nuntil Saturday afternoon.\n\nTropical Systems...\nA weak circulation, Joint Typhoon Warning Center's (JTWC) \nInvest 96W is centered just west of Wake Island near 19N166E. Invest \n96W is rated low, which means development into a tropical cyclone is \nnot expected within the next 24 hours. 96W remains very broad with a \ndisplaced wind field to the south and east side of the circulation. \nModel guidance shows 96W lifting northward, allowing the monsoon \nsurge to shift northward with it. \n\nEastern Micronesia...\nThe persistent west to southwest wind will prevail through early next\nweek, with only isolated showers expected. Winds will be light to\ngentle, with a west swell at Pohnpei, mainly southwest swell at\nKosrae, and south to southeast swell at Majuro. These marine\nconditions will prevail into next week as well.\n\nWestern Micronesia...\nDry times are on tap in Western Micronesia, likely for the next week,\npotentially for the next two weeks. Looks for either isolated or no\nshowers during this time. Enjoy the outdoors during the drier \nweather, and hopefully it doesn't lead to a drought quite yet. We\nwill probably have more than enough of that next year as the El Nino\nbegins to decay.\n\nMonsoonal westerlies look likely to continue at around the 10 to 20\nknot level, possibly 5 to 15 at Chuuk. Maybe for the second half of\nthe month the pattern will shift.\n\nWe will have a substantial north swell as Typhoon Dolphin passes well\nnorth of the area, moving from east to west on its way toward Okinawa\nand then possibly either the Chinese coast or the Korean Peninsula.\nModels then show another push of north swell about a week later,\npossibly from a future tropical system.",
